{
  "dom_id": "step-t4860",
  "name": "When a Linear Diophantine Equation Has No Solutions",
  "problem": "The linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: | \\: c,$ where $d = \\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nLet's use the theorem to test some Diophantine equations.\n\n- Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2x+10y=17.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=2,$ $b=10,$ and $c=17.$ Notice that\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(2,10)=2\n$$\n\nand $2 \\!\\not{|}\\: 17.$ Therefore, according to the theorem, our equation has no solution.\n\nIndeed, notice that the expression on the left-hand side ($2x+10y$) is always an even number, while the right-hand side of the equation ($17$) is an odd number. As a result, there is no solution. \n\n- Consider the equation\n\n$$\n4x+5y=7.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$ Since \n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(4,5)=1\n$$\n\nand $1 \\:{|}\\: 7,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nIndeed, one of the solutions for $4x+5y=7$ is the pair $(3,-1).$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
